issue_context.body:
BitConverter has APIs to convert float -> int, but not `uint`. Sometimes it's useful to get the `uint` bits underlying a float too (for ex: I'd find it useful for https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/936).

API:
``` csharp
public static unsafe ulong DoubleToUInt64Bits(double value);
public static unsafe double UInt64BitsToDouble(ulong value)

public static unsafe ushort HalfToUInt16Bits(Half value);
public static unsafe Half UInt16BitsToHalf(ushort value)

public static unsafe uint SingleToUInt32Bits(float value);
public static unsafe float UInt32BitsToSingle(uint value)
```

Note: `uint` is not CLS compliant

cc @tannergooding

comments:
In many cases for `float`/`double` it also isn't about micro-optimization, but instead correctness as you internally manipulate the parts using shifts and you don't want the sign being carried.
In order to implement `Half` for example, we will have to provide software fallbacks that manipulate the raw bits of each and that will in turn require a number of additional casts to be inserted hindering readability of already complex code.
S.P.Corelib itself wouldn't be blocked as we can just define an internal helper but if we are already defining it, and its a trivial function that would provide convenience to 3rd party users as well, then I don't see why we shouldn't also propose it as a more general API.

It's also worth noting it isn't always just casting to uint, if you are using any kind of literal then C# will require you to insert an `unchecked()` as well (due to the sign bit being set):
```csharp
uint bits = (uint)BitConverter.SingleToInt32Bits(value);
float value = BitConverter.Int32BitsToSingle(unchecked((int)0x80000000));
// vs
uint bits = BitConverter.SingleToUInt32Bits(value);
float value = BitConverter.UInt32BitsToSingle(0x80000000);
```

Would it also make sense to add the equivalents for `double`?
```cs
public static unsafe ulong DoubleToUInt64Bits(double value);
public static unsafe double UInt64BitsToDouble(ulong value);
```

[Video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ynpmIT-DlO0&t=0h5m1s)

* Looks good as proposed

```C#
namespace System
{
    public static class BitConverter
    {
        public static unsafe ulong DoubleToUInt64Bits(double value);
        public static unsafe double UInt64BitsToDouble(ulong value);

        public static unsafe ushort HalfToUInt16Bits(Half value);
        public static unsafe Half UInt16BitsToHalf(ushort value);

        public static unsafe uint SingleToUInt32Bits(float value);
        public static unsafe float UInt32BitsToSingle(uint value);
    }
}
```
